-- title:
--   Component maps of the Raynaud quotient on the special fibre
-- statement:
--   Arithmetic setting. Fixed are a prime $p$ and a natural number $M \neq 0$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ such that every unit of $\mathbb{Z}/M$ whose image under `ZMod.unitsMap` for the divisibility $M/p \mid M$ is $1$ lies in $H$ (hypothesis `hHp`), and $M/p \neq 0$. Further fixed are a valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with `Pl.LiesOverPrime p`, i.e. $p$ lies in the nonunits of $Pl$, whose residue field is of characteristic $p$ and algebraically closed, and the hypothesis `hj` that the $q$-series `jqModC ℚ` lies in the $q$-expansion function field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`, which allows the two-chart integral models `toBase p Γ hj` to be formed.
--
--   Models and Néron objects. $\mathfrak{X}$ is an integral model package `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` for the levels $\Gamma_M(H)$ and $\Gamma_N$; $\Lambda$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData p M H hpM Pl`, consisting of a morphism $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ from $\operatorname{Spec} Pl$ to `base p` compatible with the generic point, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ with structure morphism $\Lambda.f$ to `base p`, a relative group law $\Lambda.L$ on $\Lambda.f$, and bijections of $J_H(M/p)$ with the sections over the generic point and of the degree-zero Picard group of the special-fibre function field with the sections over the residue point; and $O$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`, comprising a scheme $O.G$ with structure morphism $O.g$ to `base p`, a commutative relative group law $O.L$, a bijection `O.pts` of $J_H(M,H) = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` with the sections of $O.g$ over the generic point which is additive and Galois-equivariant, the geometric hypotheses on $O.g$ (smooth, separated, locally of finite type, quasi-compact, surjective, with preconnected fibres), Hecke endomorphisms compatible with `genOpH`, flatness and surjectivity of multiplication by $n$, a toric rank `O.toricRank`, and the subgroups `O.toricPts m` (generated by the toric points for $m>0$, trivial otherwise) and `O.finPts m` (generated by those $m$-torsion classes in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ whose associated section satisfies `ExtendsToPlace Pl Λ.σA`). The hypotheses `hrep` and `hrepΛ` assert that the designations built from $(O.G, O.g)$ and from $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$ with their unit sections represent the relative sub-Picard functors cut out by `algEquivZeroCut`, that is by the fibrewise algebraically-trivial condition, on `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ and on `toBase p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj` rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$.
--
--   The local base ring. $Rh$ is a henselian local domain which is a discrete valuation ring, equipped with an algebra structure over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with faithful scalar multiplication such that all images lie in $Pl$ (`hRA`) and such that the maximal ideal of $Rh$ consists exactly of the elements of $Pl$-valuation $<1$ (`hRloc`), and with an algebra map to $\mathbb{Z}/p$ whose kernel is again described by $Pl$-valuation $<1$ (`hres`). A set $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ and a unit $d$ of $\mathbb{Z}/M$ are fixed, the latter with the hypothesis `hd` that the image of $d$ in $\mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$ equals $p$.
--
--   The $p$-divisible group of the finite part. $\mathcal{G}$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup Rh p h`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199), i.e. a tower of finite free cocommutative Hopf $Rh$-algebras `𝒢.level v` of rank $p^{vh}$ with surjective transition maps whose kernels are the indicated torsion ideals. It comes with an additive map $\Delta$ from $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{Points}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ to $J_H(M,H)$ and a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map $e$ on Tate modules, subject to: injectivity of $\Delta$ (`hΔinj`); `hΔlev`, that $y$ lies in `O.finPts (p ^ v)` if and only if $y$ is $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point; Galois equivariance `hΔgal` for pairs of automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ agreeing as maps; `hΔhecke`, that each Hecke generator [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) is realised on $\Delta$-images by a transition-compatible system of bialgebra endomorphisms of the levels; `he`, that $e$ is given coordinatewise by $\Delta$; injectivity `heinj`; `herange`, that a Tate vector lies in the range of $e$ exactly when its $n$-th coordinate lies in `O.finPts (p ^ n)` for all $n$; Galois equivariance `hegal` relating `𝒢.tateModuleRep` and `JH.tateGaloisRep`; saturation `hsat` of the range of $e$ under multiplication by $p$; `hcoker`, the existence of a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear isomorphism of the cokernel of $e$ with $\mathbb{Z}_p^{O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$; and `htor`, that every element of `O.toricPts (p ^ v)` is $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point.
--
--   The subquotient $\mathcal{B}$. $\mathcal{B}$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup Rh p hB`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) with levelwise bialgebra maps $\psi_v : \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, together with numerical hypotheses $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + hB$ (`hhB`) and $hB = 2h'$ (`hhB2`), and: `hψt`, compatibility of $\psi$ with the transition maps; `hψker`, that the point obtained from a $\mathcal{G}$-point $x$ by composing with $\psi_v$ is trivial precisely when $\Delta(x)$ lies in `O.toricPts (p ^ v)`; `hψsurj`, surjectivity of $\psi_v$ on points over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hψred`, that if the $\psi_v$-image of $x$ is congruent to the counit modulo valuation $<1$ on all of $\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, then so is $x$ on $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$; and `hperiod`, that for $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, for $z$ a $p^v$-torsion class of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` and $y$ a level-$v$ point with $\Delta(y) = \sigma \cdot z - z$, the $\psi_v$-image of $y$ is congruent to the counit modulo valuation $<1$.
--
--   The immersions over $Rh$. $\rho_h$ is a ring map from `R p` to $Rh$ and $\iota_v$ a morphism $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$ for each $v$, subject to: `hρh`, compatibility of the algebra maps into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hιbase`, that $\iota_v$ followed by $O.g$ is the base morphism; `hιcl`, that the induced map into the fibre product of $O.g$ and $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$ is a closed immersion; `hιp`, that $\iota_v$ is killed by multiplication by $p^v$; `hιpts`, that $\iota_v$ recovers `O.pts ∘ Δ` on points; `hιmul`, that $\iota_v$ is a homomorphism for $O.L$ on points over any $Rh$-algebra; `hιt`, compatibility with the transition maps; `hιhecke`, that each Hecke generator is realised by transition-compatible bialgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ with $\operatorname{Spec}\varphi_v$ followed by $\iota_v$ equal to $\iota_v$ followed by `(O.hecke S g).1`, and inducing `genOpH` on $\Delta$-images; and `hιfin`, which states for each $v$ (given the two compatibility equalities $h3$, $h4$) that the canonical morphism $j_v$ from $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v)$ into the iterated fibre product of the $p^v$-kernel of $O.L$ with $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$ is both an open and a closed immersion, and that every point of that fibre product whose image under the second projection is the closed point of $Rh$ lies in the range of $j_v$ on points.
--
--   The special fibre. $\sigma_p$ is a morphism $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p) \to$ `base p` with `hσp` identifying it with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $Rh \to \mathbb{Z}/p$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$; $\iota^p_v$ is a morphism $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{Rh} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to$ the fibre product of $O.g$ and $\sigma_p$, whose two projections are prescribed by `hιp₁` (the first is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the right inclusion followed by $\iota_v$) and `hιp₂` (the second is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the structure map of $\mathbb{Z}/p$). A pair $q_0, q_1$ of morphisms over $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p)$ from the base change of $O.g$ along $\sigma_p$ to the base change of $\Lambda.f$ along $\sigma_p$ is given, together with: an algebra structure of the residue field of $Pl$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ and `hfac`, the factorisation of $\operatorname{Spec}$ of that algebra map followed by $\sigma_p$ as `resPt Pl` followed by $\Lambda.\sigma_A$; `hqmul`, that each $q_i$ is a homomorphism for the base-changed group laws on test objects over $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p)$; and `hqbc`, that the two morphisms `O.abqFibre i` over the residue field agree with $q_i$ after the base-change comparison maps induced by $\mathbb{Z}/p \to \mathrm{ResidueField}\,Pl$. The hypothesis `hjB` asserts that $1 \otimes \psi_v$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ is injective for every $v$.
--
--   The level group $\mathcal{A}$. $\mathcal{A}$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup (baseRing p) p hΛ`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) with $hB = 2h\Lambda$ (`hBΛ`), an algebra structure of $\mathbb{Z}/p$ over `baseRing p` with `hσp'` identifying $\sigma_p$ with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of that algebra map, and morphisms $\iota'_v$ from $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the $v$-th level of the base change $\mathcal{A} \otimes \mathbb{Z}/p$ to the fibre product of $\Lambda.f$ and $\sigma_p$, subject to: `hι'base`, prescribing the second projection; `hι'cl`, that each $\iota'_v$ is a closed immersion; `hι'p`, that the induced map onto the $p^v$-kernel of the base-changed group law $\Lambda.L$ is an isomorphism; `hι'mul`, that $\iota'_v$ is a homomorphism for that group law on points over any $\mathbb{Z}/p$-algebra; and `hι't`, compatibility with the transition maps.
--
--   Conclusion. For every $v \in \mathbb{N}$ there exist morphisms $r_i : \operatorname{Spec}$ of the $v$-th level of $\mathcal{B} \otimes \mathbb{Z}/p$ into the fibre product of $\Lambda.f$ and $\sigma_p$, for $i \in \{0,1\}$, and bialgebra maps $\rho_i$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ from the $v$-th level of $\mathcal{A} \otimes \mathbb{Z}/p$ to the $v$-th level of $\mathcal{B} \otimes \mathbb{Z}/p$, such that both of the following hold: first, for each $i$, $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the ring map underlying $1 \otimes \psi_v : \mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{Rh} \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{Rh} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, followed by $r_i$, equals $\iota^p_v$ followed by $q_i$; second, for each $i$, $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_i$ followed by $\iota'_v$ equals $r_i$.
--
--   This is the step, in the analysis of the special fibre at $p$ of the Néron object attached to $J_H(M,H)$, which produces the two component maps of the Raynaud quotient of the finite part as morphisms into the $p^v$-torsion of the level-$(M/p)$ object, and lifts them to bialgebra maps between the corresponding levels of the $p$-divisible groups over $\mathbb{F}_p$. It is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_bialgEquiv_baseChange_raynaudQuotient_tensorProduct_levelTorsion_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_bialgEquiv_baseChange_raynaudQuotient_tensorProduct_levelTorsion_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness), where these component maps are assembled into an isomorphism.
